.TH CWE-645 7 "2026-11-15" "CWE Top 25 (2025)" "Software Weakness Reference"
.SH NAME
CWE\-645 \- Overly Restrictive Account Lockout Mechanism
.SH RANK
KEV\-referenced weakness. Not part of the 2025 CWE Top 25; documented here because it appears in the CISA KEV catalog.
.SH CLASSIFICATION
Class: KEV\-referenced weakness.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The product contains an account lockout protection mechanism, but the mechanism is too restrictive and can be triggered too easily, which allows attackers to deny service to legitimate users by causing their accounts to be locked out. Account lockout is a security feature often present in applications as a countermeasure to the brute force attack on the password based authentication mechanism of the system. After a certain number of failed login attempts, the users' account may be disabled for a certain period of time or until it is unlocked by an administrator. Other security events may also possibly trigger account lockout. However, an attacker may use this very security feature to deny service to legitimate system users. It is therefore important to ensure that the account lockout security mechanism is not overly restrictive.
.SH KNOWN EXPLOITED VULNERABILITIES
CISA KEV catalog (as of 2026-11-15): \fB1\fR exploited CVE mapped to this weakness. Each entry has the catalog write\-up of how it was used in the wild (not a runnable exploit) and the required remediation. Code\-level vulnerable\->fixed pairs, when curated, are under \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.SS CVE\-2023\-4346
KNX Association KNX Protocol Connection Authorization Option 1, added 2026\-07\-15.
.PP
KNX Association KNX Protocol Connection Authorization Option 1 Overly Restrictive Account Lockout Mechanism Vulnerability.
.PP
\fIHow it is exploited (CISA):\fR
KNX Association KNX Protocol Connection Authorization Option 1 contains an overly restrictive account lockout mechanism vulnerability that could allow an attacker to purge all devices without additional security options enabled and set a BCU key to lock the device.
.PP
\fIRemediation:\fR
Apply vendor mitigations; follow CISA BOD 26\-04 (risk\-based patching) and forensics triage. Discontinue use if no mitigation exists.
 Due 2026\-07\-29.
.PP
